I got extremely frustrated with Nethack when I first started. I had had 
little experience (and long ago) with D&D, and no other role-playing or 
roguelike game experience at all. I rarely made it down to the Oracle, 
and almost never had much money when I did, so getting tips from her was 
pretty much out of the question. I most frequently played Wizards, and 
starved a lot.

I did what I imagine most people do: just tried out stuff to see what 
would happen. I collapsed in laughter the first time I hallucinated. I 
quaffed from fountains, not expecting a wish, but just because I had a 
vague idea that my character could die from thirst, as well as hunger. I 
quickly learned not to do that. I also eschewed the Mines, which 
frequently killed my character much more quickly than the main dungeon.

I also didn't for the longest time know how to bless or curse things. I 
knew that some things *were* blessed and cursed, but I didn't have the 
foggiest idea of how to change the status. I also didn't get the concept 
when I first came across wishing. I didn't want to wish for an *object*; 
I wanted to wish for experience levels, or hit points, or something 
intangible that would let me get a handle on the game. The first time or 
two, being limited to wishing for objects, I think I wished for food 
rations.

I had originally come across Nethack from a Debian Potato distribution, 
which also included Dylan's spoilers. Out of frustration, I decided to 
look into them, but at that stage, all the tables didn't mean much to 
me. What I needed were pointers, like the magical properties of holy 
water, or what prayer would do for you.

Eventually I googled for more info, and came up with a short spoiler 
called (iirc) "How to Win at Nethack," and then Mikko Saari's wonderful 
"Absolute Beginner's Guide." That was probably the single most helpful 
document I read. That, in turn, turned me on to r.g.r.n. I lurked here 
for a while, and learned of mysterious and wonderful places like an 
underground Castle and a fort, and of a region called Gehennom that 
nobody liked. I also learned about Elbereth, which I at first used to 
great advantage (finally, a relief from soldier ants!) and then quit 
using when I got good enough to do without it.

And once I could post YAFAP, about a year and a half into playing the 
game, I finally felt that I could adequately contribute a bit. And so 
here I am.

